//will add more to readme//


Setup:
Requires JavaFX and Maven to be installed.


Engine:
The engine uses an array of longs to represent the grid. The cells
are selected with a sequence of bit shifts. The cells are then 
updated via a precalculated lookup table of possible cell neighbors. 
Most of this has been somewhat abstracted through the use of the 
ArrayHelpers class. The engine allows for both multi and single threading.
The single threaded mode is a basic run through of every row on the grid.
The multi threaded mode initializes a thread pool and delegates each a certain
chunk of rows depending on the grid size and number of availabe threads.
The user is able to pause the update process, restart it, and change the speed.



GUI:
The UI is done primarily through JavaFX and FXML. The actual rendering of the 
grid is done using a PixelBuffer inside the Renderer class(Also note the DemoPlayer
for the title utilizes essentially the same rendering routine, just slightly different
to allow for effects and other settings). The renderer has in engine zooming and panning. 
Zooming also adjusts the viewport to only render the visible area. The panning 
utilizes a velocity vector to smooth out movements. The top section of the game
screen has a section for game controls. It also allows the user to change the current palette
used when rendering the grid. The main menu is layed out in the menu FXML file. 
It lets the user change the initial grid size, and the threading mode used for the engine. 
The background of the main menu is just a prerendered loop of a given simulation (made it randomize 
the palette as well).


Controls:
(Also available inside the Settings tabe on the main menu)
Pan Camera - LEFT CLICK
Zoom Camera - MouseWheel/Q/E
Quick Restart - R
Quick Pause - SPACE